Orin's space-time-scope was a reality! Technically, the STS broke the laws of optics. Orin's design utilized time and complex predictive modeling to trade frame rate for resolution. On a typical 48 megapixel camera, each pixel is approximately 1 micrometer wide. Moving the sensor ever so slightly with respect to a desired frame of reference allowed him to greatly increase the effective resolution of composite images.

At 2 um/ms, Orin could move his smartphone's camera sensor through 8 image positions every millisecond. Coupled with a space-time aware imaging program, Orin's new product instantly produced images with 4x image quality for relatively low cost.October 2, 2025
---------------
3I/ATLAS produced too much nickel. When it reached 2.6 AU, it also produced too much iron.

Orin had read an article by Avi Loeb the night he developed the STS. He quickly calculated how far each micrometer adjustment of the STS would reach at that distance. He started working on a proposal for NASA to review. The Mars Reconnaissance Orbiter had the best position for a close-up flyby: it covered an area of 30 km at 0.2 AU. Orin regretted not having published the design for his STS much sooner. The comet had only come into view a few months ago, but now he was working against the clock to convince NASA to use "an unproven technique".

<Flashback to June 17, 1998>October 3, 2025
