Orin's father, Lt. Colonel Henry Hiller ("H2"), turned 77 today. They visited the Strategic Air and Space Museum together, capturing a rare glimpse of an F-117A that had been retired in 2008. His father was a former Air Force pilot who had flown F-4s ("Rhinos") and F-16s ("Vipers") with the 4th Fighter Squadron from 1972 until he retired in 1990, just before the Gulf War.

Henry had not been briefed on the F-117A in when it entered service in 1983 (it was de-classified in 1988).

"Whew! Did you know the F117 program was started in 1975, just three years after I joined the Air Force?" Henry said as he stared at the angular black plane in front of them.

"Yes, dad. The Stealth Fighter has entertained us many times." Orin smiled - he knew his dad was about to reminisce about his years piloting F-16s.

"My last flight hours were logged on brand-new Vipers. To think as I was training fly-boys how to fly Rhinos and Vipers, there were folks squirreled away learning how to command a Wobblin' Goblin!"

The Wobbling Goblin was the pet name that drivers (pilots) assigned to the Stealth Fighter. In Lockheed's zeal to produce a bomber with a 0.003 m^2 radar cross section, they had forgotten to include features that made pilots want to fly the aircraft. The plane lacked radar (to reduce electronic noise) and only flew missions at night. Earth seemed to fight the very existence of such a thing: it was a pain to keep in the air on account of the radar-inhibiting angular design.

"Remind me, dad, what's a Rhino?" Orin asked cheekily.

"Well, Fly-Boy, Rhinos were my pride and joy - the F4 Phantom II of course! It was a damn shame when the air force retired them for those new-fangled Vipers. The fly-by-wire system sounds great on paper: 9G versus 7G and stall prevention were great features, sure...but I don't think any of the AF big wigs ever thought about landing. I've never had to fight a computer to land a Rhino!"

The F-4 had been protecting American interests for over 30 years. If Lt. Colonel Henry Hiller could have his way, McDonnell Douglas would never have been purchased by Boeing and the F-16 would have never been built.October 12, 2025
