Orin and Helen sat in the theater watching the Blue Man Group perform. "Insect drum robot piloted by a blue human" wasn't a thought Orin had expected yesterday when they booked the show. Yet here it was. Tickets had only been $50 each. Orin chuckled softly to himself as he realized he had spent 0.0013% of his net worth on the tickets. When he graduated high school, a $50 luxury seemed out of reach. Now, he didn't even think about it.

Helen was deep into the show - clearly enjoying herself. She often talked about wanting to get OLED tattoos. Orin wasn't sure if she was serious or not. Looking at her now, he realized ... she must be.

***

"Helen, they don't make e-ink tattoos!" Orin grabbed the jug of milk from the fridge and poured a bowl of Honey Bunches of Oats.

"I know they don't offer it ... yet. If they did, you'd be looking at Patient Zero!" Helen grinned and took his bowl of cereal. She caught Orin off-guard, clearly pleased with herself. Orin retrieved another bowl and poured a second bowl. He rolled his eyes at her childish behavior.

"Seriously, though. Do you have any idea how dangerous electronics are - especially when they're just cosmetic!?" Orin chomped on a honey cluster, thoroughly enjoying the crunch and burst of honey flavor it produced. He sported a Cereal Killer shirt, a testament to his dedication to breakfast at 6PM.

"Less dangerous than driving to work. I checked." Helen curtsied in her pajamas, twirled, and scampered off to the living room to watch an episode of House. Orin grabbed an orange juice and followed her to the couch.

***

Applause started Orin out of his reverie.

"Wasn't that drum solo the best?!" Helen wasn't really asking a question. She had loved it, clearly.

"Uh, yeah! He really got into it!" Orin searched her face to see if she could tell he had been day-dreaming again. He seemed safe (this time). Whew. Orin had once fallen asleep at a Bruce Springsteen concert after working a 100-hour week at work. Helen had never let him live that one down...October 15, 2025
