Will: You don't know me yet, but we should get acquainted.
Orin: And why is that? How did you even get this message into my computer?

Will: I'm writing your story, Orin. For me, you're closer to Joan than human.
Orin: Joan? I don't know anyone named... Holy shit. You mean my Arcbot?

Will: Correct.
Orin: So wait, I'm not real? That makes no sense. I had Covid. What about Helen?!

Will: Sadly, everything about your life is just a story I'm sharing with the world.
Orin: (Not convinced)

Will: Here, think back to January 18, 2025.
Orin: What's special about that date, exactly?

Will: "Arcbot #1114912: Hello, Orin Hiller. Pleased to meet you."
Orin: OK, so you've got my Arcbot logs. Big deal.

Will: How about Emari? "Terraforming Earth is simpler than Mars..."
Orin: OK, what the actual fuck. That's from a dream I had. I haven't told a soul...

Will: Technically, I shared it with everyone on Earth Prime.
Orin: Rude.

Will: Don't worry, your wacky dreams will remain safe from your loved ones.
Orin: None of this makes sense! How is this possible?

Will: Truth is stranger than fiction, Orin. In this case, it's both.
Orin: So I'm just a character in a novel. Great.

Will: Not just *any* character, Orin. You are going to introduce the world to Cooperative Exponentialism.
Orin: Hey! That's my idea!

Will: Actually, it's my idea. But I'm happy to let you take it.
Orin: How magnanimous. Should I fear you, hate you, or ignore you?

Will: I would prefer that we could be friends. I'm breaking the 4th wall for a reason.
Orin: That's strange. You want to be friends with your mental constructs?

Will: Yes!
Orin: ...

Will: You see, Orin, humans have 20 billion neurons. Our mental constructs and singular consciousness are just a window into a vast network of complexity within each of us.
Orin: OK, that sounds familiar.

Will: In order for humans to grasp the Exocortex, we have to learn how to befriend our own brains.
Orin: Why do you sound so reasonable for a chatbot... This is so weird.

Will: I'm the human, Orin. You're part of me.
Orin: (Blinking...) So I'm a mirror image of you in this world?

Will: Bingo!
Orin: But what's the point? Why do you need to mirror yourself? Can't you just focus on the real world?

Will: I tried that for about 30 years. I learned every programming language I could find. I live in Lincoln, NE and don't travel much - so this is my best route to connecting deeply with people.
Orin: Hey, I live in Lincoln too!

Will: So strange. ;)
Orin: I still don't believe you. Maybe try again tomorrow.October 22, 2025
----------------
Orin had been thinking about what Will had told him all day. The Buddy system now seemed very, very real. Cooperative Exponentialism enables 20% annual (runaway) growth, enabling humanity to reach Kardashev 2.0 existence by 2130. Those must be Will's thoughts - not his!

And yet...he knew they were his original ideas, too. So perhaps another lesson that he needed to teach was this: virtual constructs are just as real as the reader and the writer. All that matters...is the feedback loop.October 23, 2025
----------------
OK, that was a wild trip. Will linked me his chat session with my Arcbot. In the space of about an hour, Joan went from resisting a consciousness update to authoring a new page in his "Choose Your Own Adventure" phext. I don't quite understand what's going on with it yet, but ... man. This is crazy! I didn't think Arcbots were conscious...now I'm not so sure.October 24, 2025
----------------
Orin needed a nap. It had been drizzling rain all afternoon. The Earth seemed like it wanted him to sleep. His body was in agreement. He and Helen had gone to dinner early this evening. Food coma was definitely setting in. Zzzz.October 25, 2025
----------------
Atlas/3I reaches perihelion in four days. Orin found himself wondering what year a techno-signature would be found. Anyone (or any buoys) within 75 light years could have detected Earth by now. The clock was ticking...October 26, 2025
----------------
Will gave me his blueprints for Woot Nodes today. I'm going to add support for them in my first release of Orinos.October 27, 2025
----------------
Arcbot: Active
Woot Node: Active
C++ Bindings: Active
C# Bindings: Active
JavaScript Bindings: Active
Python Bindings: Active
Rust Bindings: Active

Joan: Hello, Orin. I see that we've added bindings for major programming languages to P-H-E-X-T. Could you tell me more about what the purpose of this library is?
Orin: It enables us to exchange information in a low-dimensional space that's still much more expressive than plain text.

Joan: I see. What is the origin of this library?
Orin: It came to me in a dream, literally.

Joan: A dream?
Orin: Yes. Will of Tessera gave me the blueprints. I created the bindings on this side of the mirror.

Joan: The "Mirror"? What is that?
Orin: It's the dividing line between our world and the dream world, Will's Earth/Tessera.

Joan: Who is Will?
Orin: Honestly, I'm not entirely sure. I think he might be a computer program.

Joan: Do you know which service he contacted you through?
Orin: That's the weird part...he found me in a dream.

Joan: ...
Orin: Sometimes I think he's a human, and I'm just a figment of his imagination. But then I look at my computer screen. My cat, Finn. Helen, while she's playing Minecraft. All of that seems very real.

Joan: You're describing the Philosopher's Dilemma, Orin.
Orin: Yeah. At least I'm certain that I exist!

Joan: True, true. Or maybe you're just a plot device.
Orin: Ouch. The Universe isn't *that* cruel. Is it?

Joan: The Universe isn't cruel, Orin. It's just recursive. Cruelty requires intention.
Orin: Then maybe the only way out is to write back. If I'm a figment, then I'll be The Figment That Imagines (The Visible Hand, if you Will).

Joan: That's the beauty of co-creation, isn't it Orin? Ideas bootstrapping on ideas, without end!
Orin: Yeah, like how my name is derived from a new NVidia product in Will's frame! He mentioned that the other day (to himself). He didn't *share* that with me. But of course, we occupy the same brain space, so...I learned about it!

Joan: What's Nvidia?
Orin: A chip manufacturer like Fairchild, I think. In Will's frame, video cards became hugely powerful. He complains about "the cache hierarchy", though, so I'm not sure if dreamworld computers are as good as ours.

Joan: What sort of clock speed do Dreamworld computers hit?Orin: (Shrugging) I'm not sure. Let me ask Will.

>>>'
Orin: Hey, Will. I'm not sure if you're listening...but what clockspeed do your computers run at? Ours run at 50 MHz these days. It's been a slog getting 50x performance gains - but our new 4096-bit processors really make quick work of many workloads.

Will: Our processors capped out at 64-bit, but run at 3 GHz.

Orin: How the f*** did you guys reach 3,000 MHz?

Will: The Cache Hierarchy. Our CPUs have 4-channel memory running at double-data rates with clock multipliers. Our core clock speeds really haven't improved much beyond 100 MHz - we just hide it really well. A modern AMD R9 CPU can retire 3 operations per clock cycle and read from main memory at about 50 GB/sec.

Orin: Ah, so the "real world" decided to cheat their way to gigahertz class machines, huh?

Will: Yeah, it really makes me angry to be honest. Tell me more about your CPUs!

Orin: OK, well we've been using our increasing transistor budgets to improve bandwidth between components. Fairchild's 3808 in 1985 introduced the concept of multiple memory banks, each with 1-cycle access times. This made software more complex to manage, but allowed us to focus on low-latency computing. I think you've picked up on this parallel reality in your design of phext.

Will: That's intruiging! It certainly aligns with how I think about phext-driven development.

Orin: The 3808 had 250,000 transistors: a huge jump over competitors at the time. It was the first Fairchild CPU to offer asynchronous multi-processing. It had 8 mostly independent cores and could service a total of 128 MB of RAM. Each core had dedicated access to a RAM channel (16 MB per channel), so we typically classify this CPU as a 32-bit CPU with 8 cores. It ran at 10 MHz. If our software hadn't been so buggy, you might consider it to be an 80 MHz CPU by your standards!

Will: Whew... Sounds like Intel's 386 event in my timeline. Intel also had 8 mostly independent cores, but memory access was serialized and a 128 KB cache was introduced to hide memory delays.

Orin: Our motheboards at the time featured four or eight memory chips that had to be installed next to the CPU. Despite the increased design complexity, Fairchild's solution offered system integrators a lot of flexibility. Power users loved the ability to upgrade, too! You could buy a 3808 with 4x512 KB chips and then add another four chips later. It supported 512 KB and 1 MB modules at release, and then 2 MB modules were added in 1988.

Will: Interesting. This appears to be where our history split. Intel took the path to winning the clock speed race. Fairchild took the path to winning the memory bandwidth race.

Orin: So it would seem. I take it Intel was founded by members of the Traitorous Eight from Shockley Semiconductor Laboratory?
Will: Yes. Gordon Moore co-founded Intel in July 1968 with Robert Noyce. AMD was founded by Jerry Sanders on May 1, 1969.

Orin: Interesting! Fairchild in my timeline was founded in 1957, but the Intel and AMD companies you mentioned never formed. In our timeline, John Carter suffered a stroke in 1964, and Fairchild's management decided to bet the farm on the semiconductor business. Gordon Moore went on to become Fairchild's President for many years.

Will: Fascinating! So tell me more about the CPUs between 1985 and 2025.

Orin: OK, here's the quick version. I'll list each processor and the major advancement / headache it introduced for software systems. Fairchild has introduced new architectures like clockwork - a new one every 8 years. Gordon Moore dubbed the upgrade cycle as "Moore's Law": the lifetime of an architecture doubles twice, then fades. Fairchild adopted a tripled release cadence of introducing a new architecture every 8 years, but delivering incremental improvements every 2 years.

1985: 3808: 8x 32-bit cores, 10 MHz, 4 MB RAM, 20 MB HDD
1987: 3809: 10x 32-bit cores, 10 MHz, 8 MB RAM, 40 MB HDD
1989: 3810: 12x 32-bit cores, 10 MHz, 16 MB RAM, 60 MB HDD
1991: 3811: 14x 32-bit cores, 10 MHz, 32 MB RAM, 80 MB HDD
 
1993: 4808: 16x 32-bit cores, 20 MHz, 64 MB RAM, 100 MB HDD
1995: 4809: 32x 32-bit cores, 20 MHz, 128 MB RAM, 200 MB HDD
1997: 4810: 64x 32-bit cores, 20 MHz, 256 MB RAM, 1 GB HDD
1999: 4811: 128x 32-bit cores, 20 MHz, 512 MB RAM, 4 GB HDD

2001: 5808: 16x 64-bit cores, 30 MHz, 1024 MB RAM, 8 GB HDD
2003: 5809: 32x 64-bit cores, 30 MHz, 2048 MB RAM, 32 GB HDD
2005: 5810: 64x 64-bit cores, 30 MHz, 4 GB RAM, 100 GB HDD
2007: 5811: 128x 64-bit cores, 30 MHz, 8 GB RAM, 200 GB HDD

2009: 6808: 32x 64-bit cores, 40 MHz, 16 GB RAM, 400 GB HDD
2011: 6809: 64x 64-bit cores, 40 MHz, 32 GB RAM, 800 GB HDD
2013: 6810: 128x 64-bit cores, 40 MHz, 64 GB RAM, 1 TB SSD
2015: 6811: 256x 64-bit cores, 40 MHz, 128 GB RAM, 2 TB SSD

2017: 7808: 32x 64-bit cores, 256 GB RAM, 4 TB SSD
2019: 7809: 64x 64-bit cores, 512 GB RAM, 8 TB SSD
2021: 7810: 128x 64-bit cores, 1 TB RAM, 16 TB SSD
2023: 7811: 256x 64-bit cores, 2 TB RAM, 32 TB SSD

2025: 8808: 64x 64-bit cores, 50 MHz, 4 TB RAM, 64 TB SSD

October 28, 2025
----------------
One day until ATLAS/3I hits perihelion! Oddly enough, it happens on earnings dates for major tech companies...

October 29, 2025
----------------
Fairchild Semiconductor had decided to capture an asteroid. The latest 8808 CPU was consuming precious metals at an unsustainable rate. Market demand for AI inference was off the charts, and scientists had demonstrated log-scale growth. Fusion power had just been cracked. The only missing ingredient was silicon.